# Fleet choices about the mission itself, and the approach mystery thread.
# Arc and plants: content/STORY_BIBLE.md (spoilers). Voice: content/STYLE.md.

card dec_halfway
type decision
cat mission
days 19 23
weight 14
once
duration 1
roles A B
lean warm feast quiet
text Halfway, by the flight plan's clock. {A} wants to break out the good rations and mark it properly. {B} would rather not count yet.
option feast | Mark it | The fleet eats the good rations on seven screens at once. {A} gives a toast about the halfway mark, and it turns into a toast about the people who packed the food.
  res food -2
  morale A 4
  morale B 2
option quiet | Let it pass | Halfway passes without a word on the fleet channel. On every ship, at the same minute, someone looks at the clock anyway.
  bond A B 3

card dec_name
type decision
cat mission
days 26 34
weight 10
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious after home
text The backup-site maps, stitched from imagery older than the cargo landings, show an unnamed crater small enough that the fleet gets to suggest a name. {A} wants to name it after the first person who ever wrote a Mars mission plan. {B} wants somewhere at home.
option after | Honor the planners | The name goes in the request. Somewhere, a committee will argue about it for years, which {A} considers part of the honor.
  set name_plan
  trait A curious 2
option home | Name it for home | The crater gets the name of a small town on Earth that most of Earth has never heard of. Within a day, the town's newspaper has a front page about it.
  set name_home
  trait B warm 3
  morale B 4

card dec_science
type decision
cat mission
days 14 24
weight 8
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious record save
text A gamma-ray burst goes off far past the galaxy, and the fleet's dose monitors catch it. Timed against detectors near Earth, the data could pin the source, but sending it costs a day of the dish, and every letter waits. {A} wants the data sent. {B} wants the letters.
option record | Send the data | The burst's source shrinks from a smear of sky to a point. {A} saves the confirmation in the photo folder.
  morale B -2
  trait A curious 3
  morale A 3
option save | Letters first | Ground telescopes get the burst anyway, less sharply. {B} reads every letter, and doesn't pretend it was an easy trade.
  trait B warm 2

# --- The mystery chain (fixed days; one beat a day from 35 to 41) ------------------
card m_ripple
type event
cat mystery
clue
days 16 21
weight 14
once
roles A
when cond A ok
text {A} cleans up the receiver's noise floor for the weekly report and finds a faint ripple in it, at about nine hundredths of a hertz. A control loop hunting somewhere, most likely the coolant pump's: it's on the same bus. {A} notes it in the margin and moves on to the propellant tables.
do trait A curious 1

card m_dropout
type event
cat mystery
clue core
due
once
days 20 23
roles A
when since t_carrier >= 5
text {A} plots a week of the old-channel carrier for the anomaly file. It isn't steady: there for half the day, gone for the rest, and back thirty-seven minutes later each morning. {A} blames the dish's scan schedule, re-plots it, and the thirty-seven minutes stay.
do mark t_dropout

card m_theory
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 22 24
roles A
when since t_dropout >= 2
when !is A ow_list
when !is A ow_song
when !is A ow_garden
when !is A ow_letter
when !is A ow_dose
when !is A ow_hail
text {A} finds the grey relay from the manifest in the catalog: spinning since its thruster stuck, one turn every 10.96 seconds, going by RF flashes heard in the weeks after it failed. Its beam, eight and a half degrees wide, is 2.4 percent of a circle. A lighthouse, unkept. {A} draws it in blue marker.
do who ow_theory A
do mark t_theory
do morale A 3

card m_theory_confirm
type event
cat mystery
clue
due
once
days 25 27
roles A
when since t_theory >= 3
when is A ow_theory
text Mission Control runs the grey relay's orbit forward for {A}: it comes round thirty-seven minutes later each morning, and slips behind Mars for an hour or so. Not half a day. Close enough, {A} decides. The ground sends a drawing of a lighthouse; {A} adds a keeper in blue and sleeps through the night.
do morale A 2

card c_theory
type beat
cat human
due
once
days 38 38
roles A
when is A ow_theory
text {A} takes the lighthouse drawing down for the entry checklist and stops. A relay would drag the knocks early and late by most of a tenth of a second each orbit, and never hide for half a day. These swing by a hundredth, once a day. Under the keeper, in blue: "Wrong lighthouse." Then: "Same keeper?"
do trait A curious 2

card m_flash1
type event
cat mystery
clue core
due
once
days 30 31
roles A
when !is A ow_theory
text {A} pulls the orbiters' night frames of the landing region, to look at the neighbourhood before moving in. On dark ground near the edge of one frame, one pixel is lit. On the next frame it isn't. A cosmic-ray hit on the sensor, almost certainly. {A} writes down both timestamps anyway.
do set flash_seen
do mark t_flash
do who flash_who A
do trait A curious 2

card m_flash2
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 32 33
roles A
when flag flash_seen
when since t_flash >= 2
when is A flash_who
text {A} goes through every night frame of that patch this month. Every pass, on different rows of the sensor, the lit pixel lands on the same spot of ground. Cosmic rays don't return to one address; a hot pixel would follow the camera, not Mars. {A} sends the list round, asking to be told it's wrong.
do set flash_twice
do open dec_flash

card dec_flash
type decision
cat mystery
days 32 33
weight 1
once
duration 1
roles A B
when flag flash_twice
lean curious look leave
text {A} wants to ask the ground to task an orbiter for a fast sequence over the lit pixel, at the cost of a day of the fleet's downlink: every letter from home waits. {B} would leave it for the people who'll be down there with time to look.
option look | Ask for the sequence | The request goes up. {A} writes the apology to six inboxes personally, and means each one.
  set flash_looked
  morale A -2
  morale B -2
option leave | Leave it for the ground | The pixel goes into the anomaly log, under a heading {B} writes and then underlines. The letters from home arrive on time, all of them.
  set flash_left
  morale B 2

card m_key
type event
cat mystery
clue core key
due
once
days 34 34
roles A
when !fired h_dark
text {A} finds a link request from Mars in the log, signed with an old program key the fleet trusts. Sequence number 1, as if the sender had just been switched on; the challenge, new. The comms computer hung on it until a watchdog cleared it. Mission Control: "Not ours." A key-rotation ticket opens.
do trait A curious 2

card flash_answer
type event
cat mystery
clue core ground
due
once
days 35 36
roles A
when flag flash_looked
when since t_flash >= 5
when is A flash_who
text The fast sequence comes down overnight: forty frames of dark ground as the orbiter slides over. The pixel lights, goes out, lights again, and never moves against the rocks. Whatever it is, it isn't in orbit. {A} counts the frames between flashes, gets the same number each time, and calls the fleet.
do set flash_ground
do trait A curious 4

card flash_answer_ground
type event
cat mystery
clue core ground
due
once
days 35 36
roles A
when flag flash_left
when since t_flash >= 5
when is A flash_who
text An email reaches {A} from someone on the orbiter team who slipped the fast sequence into a gap in the plan: forty frames of dark ground, the pixel lighting, going out, lighting again, never moving against the rocks. Whatever it is, it isn't in orbit. The email ends: "You didn't hear this from me."
do set flash_ground
do trait A curious 3

card m_overlay
type event
cat mystery
clue core
due
once
days 36 37
roles A
when flag flash_ground
when is A ow_hail
text {A} takes the old-channel receiver log, slides it back by the light time from Mars, about twenty-five seconds, and lays it over the fast sequence. The carrier peaks on the exact frame the pixel lights. Every time. It isn't two things. {A} puts both traces on the galley screen, side by side.
do trait A curious 3

card m_checked
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 37 38
roles A
when fired m_overlay
when flag flash_looked
text Mission Control answers the overlay: nothing near that pixel still has power. The nearest entry is the crash site catalogued for Cargo 2, kilometres off, written off after six weeks of ground hails and no acknowledgement. Crash sites don't blink. It goes on the next survey anyway.
do morale A 1

card m_checked_g
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 37 38
roles A
when fired m_overlay
when flag flash_left
text Mission Control answers the overlay: nothing near that pixel still has power. The nearest entry is the crash site catalogued for Cargo 2, kilometres off, written off after six weeks of ground hails and no acknowledgement. Crash sites don't blink. The orbiter-team stranger adds: "Surveying it."
do morale A 1

card m_listen
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 38 38
roles A
when fired m_overlay
when is A ow_song
text With Mars under three weeks out, the old-channel carrier is loud enough on the dish to hear without folding. {A} pipes it into the cabin speaker: a click, a hiss, a long pause, a click. The four bars fit between the clicks. {A} plays them once on the galley keyboard and doesn't explain.
do morale A -1

card m_count
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 39 39
roles A
when !is A ow_theory
when !is A ow_hail
text {A} times the old-channel carrier on the galley clock, the way you count between lightning and thunder. Everything about the fleet's distance from Mars has changed since the first night. The gap hasn't. {A} stops counting before the number means anything.

# --- The payoff: fires on day 40 in every season ------------------------------------
card m_reveal
type event
cat mystery
reveal
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when is A ow_theory
text The first sharp pass over the backup site since before the cargo ships flew comes down. Past the prime zone, behind a crater rim, a ship stands on its legs, tilted, one solar wing half open, its dish jammed against its stop: Cargo 2. From straight above it was always two pixels and a shadow. In the night video its floodlights flare at power-up, die, flare again. {A} hums the galley chime; it fits the gap.
do trait A curious 3

card m_reveal_any
type event
cat mystery
reveal
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when !fired m_reveal
text The first sharp pass over the backup site since before the cargo ships flew comes down. Past the prime zone, behind a crater rim, a ship stands on its legs, tilted, one solar wing half open, its dish jammed against its stop: Cargo 2. From straight above it was always two pixels and a shadow. In the night video its floodlights flare at power-up, die, flare again. {A} hums the galley chime; it fits the gap.
do trait A curious 3

card a_report
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when fired m_reveal
when is A flash_who
text {A} pulls up the report that wrote Cargo 2 off: a page and a half. The grey relay's thruster stuck open on the burn to cover its landing pass. Last packet: X-band amplifier OVERTEMP. Six weeks of ground hails, no acknowledgement; an old-band carrier, attributed to relay tumble. Ticket closed. {A}'s own report runs forty pages by midnight. Page one: watchdog. Page two: what lives in RAM.

card a_report_any
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when !fired a_report
when !is A ow_theory
text {A} pulls up the report that wrote Cargo 2 off: a page and a half. The grey relay's thruster stuck open on the burn to cover its landing pass. Last packet: X-band amplifier OVERTEMP. Six weeks of ground hails, no acknowledgement; an old-band carrier, attributed to relay tumble. Ticket closed. {A}'s own report runs forty pages by midnight. Page one: watchdog. Page two: what lives in RAM.

# --- Day 41: the window, one card per carrier branch --------------------------------
card a_window_hail
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag signal_answered
when is A ow_hail
text 04:10:07, fleet time: Cargo 2 knocks. All night the round trip has been shrinking toward the edge of its window. {A}'s receiver hears the knock 5.4 seconds later and answers on its own, signed, the way it has since the vote. The answer lands 10.94 seconds after the knock, on the beat, in real time, twenty milliseconds inside its window. {A} says "Hi," out loud, ten seconds early. The light stops blinking.
do set c2_answered
do morale A 8

card a_log
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when fired a_window_hail
when !is A ow_hail
text Then Cargo 2 sends its event recorder down the old channel, two years of it. From the first weeks after landing, every hello Earth sent up the normal uplink, logged the same way: HELLO RX · REPLAY · DISCARD. Then most of a million from the fleet since the vote, every one late. At the bottom, one new line: HELLO RX · OK. {A} reads it out to the fleet, slowly.
do morale A 2

card a_window_quiet
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag signal_ignored
when is A ow_hail
text 04:10:07, fleet time: Cargo 2 knocks. Nothing on the fleet's schedule answers. An answer from Earth would land minutes after the knock gave up. The fleet's would take ten seconds. The knock lasts eleven. Mission Control asks the fleet to hold anyway: no one knows what a two-year-old fault tree does with its first good handshake, and seven ships are about to land beside it.
do morale A -2

# --- Day 41: the descent brief, one per arrival branch ----------------------------
card a_descent_hold
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag arrive_hold
when is A abort_who
text The descent brief goes up four days out. The storm fills the prime zone rim to rim, so the plan retargets to the backup site and gains a line no one has had to write before: "radar for height, Cargo 2 for place". {A} reads it to the empty cabin, then runs the manual abort once, from memory.
do morale A 2

card a_descent_wait
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag arrive_wait
when !is A ow_hail
text In four days the fleet dips into the upper atmosphere instead of landing through it, into the long orbit, to wait. Below, the storm lies over the landing zone like a hand over a mouth. {A} finds the light on the night frames, fainter in the dust, still on time, and sits down with the ration tables.
do morale A -2

card a_descent_clear
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when !flag arrive_hold
when !flag arrive_wait
when !is A ow_hail
text The descent brief goes up four days out: clear air, straight in. The prime zone is exactly where the maps said, with one more thing thirty kilometres past it. {A} packs a bag for the drive out: water, a torch, spare batteries, a marker. Old habits.
do morale A 3
# Day 42: the entry itself, one scene per arrival branch, told by whoever was drilled on the manual abort.

card e_entry_hold
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 42 42
roles A
when flag arrive_hold
when is A abort_who
text Entry interface. Four minutes of plasma blackout, seven ships silent at once. At six kilometres the cameras go brown. {A}'s ship slews in a gust the model never had, and the abort call sits on {A}'s tongue, the one drilled over the link and run four days ago from memory. Then the radar finds the ground, and {A} doesn't call it.
do morale A 3


card e_entry_wait
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 42 42
roles A
when flag arrive_wait
when is A abort_who
text Aerocapture. Four minutes of plasma blackout high in the air, seven ships silent at once. Six come out the far side on time. {A}'s comes out eleven seconds late: eleven seconds of nothing on the fleet channel, the abort call ready on {A}'s tongue, as drilled. Then {A}'s voice, reading out the orbit numbers as if nothing happened.
do morale A 3


card e_entry_clear
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 42 42
roles A
when !flag arrive_hold
when !flag arrive_wait
when is A abort_who
text Entry interface in clear air. Four minutes of plasma blackout, seven ships silent at once. At the landing burn one of {A}'s engines lights late, a long half second, and the abort call is half out of {A}'s mouth, word for word as drilled over the link, when it catches. The ground comes up exactly where the maps said.
do morale A 3

